===============================================================================
NO OTHER DEPENDENCIES
===============================================================================

There are none. No npm packages, no CDN, no bundler, no build step, and no
vendored library. Every parser in this tool — GeoTIFF, Deflate, TIFF-LZW,
PackBits, EXIF, XML — is written from the published specifications in this
repository. What follows is therefore not a list of bundled code but a list of
debts.

===============================================================================
TECHNIQUES, PRIOR WORK AND DEBTS
===============================================================================

The proportional-symbol method
    Used by the circle grid and the image halftone. Marc Ihle's own technique,
    developed in 2017 and generalised here.

    It was applied at scale in "Cartography of Hadseløya" (2019) — terrain
    potentials computed from Kartverket's open data and translated into
    laser-printed physical maps, fabricated in four sizes up to 120 x 90 cm at
    Makerspace Vesterålen. Those maps are what this tool automates:
    https://digital-landscapes.com/2019/07/17/potentials-of-hadsel-island/

The DLF laser-pass layer scheme
    DLF-00_engrave … DLF-05_cut_outer, carried UNCHANGED from DL-TerrainSlicer
    and, before it, from the Grasshopper definition that tool replaced — so an
    existing Trotec JobControl configuration works as it stands.
    https://github.com/DLandscapes/dl-terrain-slicer

The interface grammar and the committed theme
    From the sibling tool DL-TerrainDiversity (Apache-2.0). The stylesheet is a
    deliberate byte-identical copy with two documented token overrides.
    https://github.com/DLandscapes/dl-terrain-diversity

Hachures
    The convention set out by Johann Georg Lehmann (1799) and developed in the
    Swiss manner: strokes down the fall line, heavier and closer where the
    ground is steeper. The same notation survives in the modern grading plan as
    the ticked embankment.

Marching squares, by triangle
    The triangular formulation is used because it has no saddle ambiguity. The
    approach is standard and long predates this implementation.

Sutherland–Hodgman polygon clipping
    Used to close region rings against the sheet edge, so a cut region meeting
    the boundary stays closed.

Horn's method
    Used for slope, because it is the derivative every GIS agrees on.

===============================================================================
FORMATS
===============================================================================

DXF is written as R12 (AC1009), a format Autodesk published openly and which
every laser front-end reads. SVG follows the W3C recommendation. Neither
implementation derives from any other codebase.

===============================================================================
DATA
===============================================================================

No sample terrain in this repository comes from a restricted source. The
demonstration site is generated by the tool itself, in code, so it cannot go
stale against the readers, and the test fixtures are synthesised.

⚠️ If you load imagery licensed for education and research only — for example
from Norge i bilder — the tool will let you PREVIEW a halftone of it and will
REFUSE to export it. That guard is deliberate. Please do not route around it.

